1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
From this to this.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
From this to this.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Transforming raw metal into civilization is a journey that began before the Bronze Age

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
and takes us into a gleaming future.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Human progress forged in the blacksmith's fire.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, Metal on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,721
Brute muscle. Forged metal.

8
00:00:34,722 --> 00:00:46,960
Combine these two elemental forces and you've got civilization itself.

9
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:52,940
The very structure of our constructed world is wrought from these basic elements.

10
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:58,200
The cadence of our progress is in the measured ring of the blacksmith's hammer.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,120
Our soaring skyscrapers, our sturdy bridges and expansive ocean liners, our jet planes and rockets.

12
00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:11,560
You can't escape the power and necessity of metal.

13
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:17,800
It is the natural material that we have reshaped into our grandest man-made structures.

14
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:24,080
We have come to a point in history when metal has really infiltrated every aspect of our life.

15
00:01:24,081 --> 00:01:31,300
It's very difficult to avoid the simple fact that it probably is the primary material that we use to extend ourselves.

16
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:38,180
Metals have characteristics that are ideal for nearly everything that makes the modern world modern.

17
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:42,700
Some conduct heat or electricity.

18
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:45,700
Others are easily stretched into wire.

19
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:48,600
Some resist cracking and breaking.

20
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:53,640
Many are shiny or exhibit enormous strength when joined with other elements.

21
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:56,300
And many of them can be worked and shaped.

22
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:59,200
Metal is a very resistant material.

23
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:05,960
Material that I have to cajole, fight, come to compromises with,

24
00:02:05,961 --> 00:02:10,200
in order to manipulate, to bend it to my will, to make what I want to make with it.

25
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:14,560
Metals are everywhere.

26
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:19,100
Not just in the obviously metallic steel pillars and aluminum cans.

27
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:23,260
Calcium in our teeth and bones.

28
00:02:23,261 --> 00:02:27,860
and silicon and glass are both metals when they are in their pure elemental state.

29
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:32,700
Metals make up two-thirds of all the known elements.

30
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:37,120
An element is made up of protons, neutrons, and electrons,

31
00:02:37,860 --> 00:02:40,800
forming an atom that cannot be reduced into other substances.

32
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:44,680
All elements are catalogued in the periodic table,

33
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:49,440
which lists each element in an ascending scale based on its atomic number,

34
00:02:49,441 --> 00:02:53,360
or number of protons and electrons making up its atoms.

35
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:56,000
And so they go in rows across the periodic table.

36
00:02:56,560 --> 00:03:00,300
And the last column in the periodic table are called the noble elements,

37
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:01,420
and those are very stable.

38
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:04,000
And so all the other elements kind of want to be like those.

39
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:09,200
And they can do that by giving up an electron or more electrons or losing electrons.

40
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:13,260
One definition of metals is that they are elements

41
00:03:13,261 --> 00:03:16,240
grouped mostly on the unstable side of the periodic table.

42
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:20,440
They are likely to give up an electron in order to bond with other elements.

43
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:25,760
The key to metals is this ability to bond with other elements and compounds

44
00:03:25,761 --> 00:03:30,800
to form substances called alloys with completely new and unique properties.

45
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:37,540
Iron, for instance, becomes ferrous oxide, or rust, when it bonds with oxygen in the air.

46
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:45,600
Because oxygen is present in the environment, we do not find most metals in their metallic form or in a pure form or in a metallic state.

47
00:03:45,601 --> 00:03:48,920
We find them in oxide form. They're bonded or linked with oxygen.

48
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:53,820
Metals alloy with lots of other elements besides oxygen.

49
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:59,420
In fact, alloys often exhibit properties that make them more than just the sum of their elemental parts.

50
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:03,300
A lot of people will talk about steel. There are lots of types of steel.

51
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:06,280
There's iron in steel, but there are other things in there as well.

52
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:09,040
And if you change that composition, you will change the properties.

53
00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:13,980
For our ancestors, defining a metal was easier.

54
00:04:13,981 --> 00:04:19,480
They knew that the shiny rocks that were often clustered in specific areas, or sometimes

55
00:04:19,481 --> 00:04:24,520
found on the ground, were often stronger than the flinty ones, and they bent rather than

56
00:04:24,521 --> 00:04:29,300
broke when pounded.

57
00:04:29,301 --> 00:04:34,880
According to archaeologists, toward the end of the Stone Age more than 6,500 years ago,

58
00:04:34,881 --> 00:04:41,220
the first metal to undergo human manipulation was likely copper, which could be found in

59
00:04:41,221 --> 00:04:46,220
deposits throughout the world, sometimes loosely embedded in the soil or in outcroppings.

60
00:04:46,221 --> 00:04:53,220
Copper is available in nature in what's called a native state, which is an unalloyed state.

61
00:04:53,221 --> 00:05:00,220
It will combine with chemicals from its surroundings to turn a bright blue or to turn a bright green,

62
00:05:00,221 --> 00:05:08,220
which made it easily identified and when in this native state could be hammered into objects relatively easily.

63
00:05:08,221 --> 00:05:12,440
They probably started to use and integrate into their lives,

64
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:15,940
and it was through the fact that it was weighty,

65
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:19,640
had weight maybe made them want to use it as an implement of some type.

66
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:22,880
And soon they learned that with the presence of heat,

67
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,220
that it was more malleable and they could form it into shapes.

68
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:27,720
And I think things just evolved from there.

69
00:05:29,740 --> 00:05:34,280
Copper was originally mined with bones and rocks to pry out the metal ore.

70
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:37,640
An ore is an amalgam of rock and metal.

71
00:05:38,220 --> 00:05:45,220
They were then able to begin to dig into the ground and follow those veins of ore.

72
00:05:45,221 --> 00:05:52,220
And eventually, a man built fires against the rock and then doused them with cold water

73
00:05:52,221 --> 00:05:57,220
in order to break the rock and extract the copper from it.

74
00:05:57,221 --> 00:06:03,220
The broken pieces of ore were gathered up and smelted, or heated, to the metal's melting temperature,

75
00:06:03,221 --> 00:06:07,220
usually in a heat-resistant ceramic container.

76
00:06:07,221 --> 00:06:15,980
In the case of copper, which melts at a relatively low 1,981 degrees Fahrenheit, that process

77
00:06:15,981 --> 00:06:16,980
wasn't too difficult.

78
00:06:16,981 --> 00:06:20,780
Today, coppersmiths follow roughly the same procedure.

79
00:06:20,781 --> 00:06:24,020
What we're doing is we're pouring an ingot.

80
00:06:24,021 --> 00:06:27,660
We have molds here that have carbon on them.

81
00:06:27,661 --> 00:06:31,900
Traditionally, they would have used well oil, burnt that to create carbon on the molds to

82
00:06:31,901 --> 00:06:35,220
release the metal once it's molten and poured into the molds themselves.

83
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:44,220
Smelting enabled us to shape copper into both tools and decorations.

84
00:06:44,221 --> 00:06:49,220
I'm looking for this nice yellow glow.

85
00:06:49,221 --> 00:06:54,220
I'm going to brush it off to make sure that I don't have any scale or dirt on there.

86
00:06:54,221 --> 00:07:00,220
And now we're going to come over and I'm going to have them start sledgehammering.

87
00:07:14,691 --> 00:07:20,220
So we're trying to get a consistent thickness through the whole billet to make sheep.

88
00:07:20,221 --> 00:07:22,220
Whoa!

89
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:36,220
Repeated hammering flattens and hardens copper by compressing the copper atoms into a tighter configuration.

90
00:07:36,221 --> 00:07:40,220
Above all, it was forging, the ability to hammer it.

91
00:07:40,221 --> 00:07:43,220
When copper is hammered, it becomes work hardened.

92
00:07:43,221 --> 00:07:46,220
And work hardening makes it stiffer, more brittle.

93
00:07:46,221 --> 00:07:53,220
It can then be hammered further into tools or decorative pieces like this.

94
00:07:56,220 --> 00:08:01,220
Copper had its limitations. It's soft and can't hold a sharp edge.

95
00:08:01,221 --> 00:08:05,220
But with the introduction of other metals came stronger copper alloys.

96
00:08:05,221 --> 00:08:10,220
No more than 9% tin could be added to the melted copper to produce the alloy bronze,

97
00:08:10,221 --> 00:08:14,220
which was harder than copper and more easily worked.

98
00:08:14,221 --> 00:08:17,548
How it was discovered will always be a mystery.

99
00:08:17,549 --> 00:08:23,920
I think it's just naturally that two materials got mixed together, perhaps lost in a fire.

100
00:08:24,440 --> 00:08:31,320
The next day they cleaned out the fire pit and there was something that was once a copper object and now was something different.

101
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:38,000
Early civilizations in both China and the Middle East were the first to forge bronze.

102
00:08:38,001 --> 00:08:45,540
As the technology traveled along trade routes, it radiated out from those areas as early as 7,000 years ago.

103
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:49,920
The usefulness of bronze, particularly for weapons such as swords and axes,

104
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:57,080
prompted historians to refer to this first stage of our large-scale use of metals as the Bronze Age.

105
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:04,440
It had such a significant impact on the quality of life for humans at that time.

106
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:06,640
It just improved their ability to survive.

107
00:09:06,641 --> 00:09:11,760
It was an insurance policy in a lot of ways that gave them an extension of their will.

108
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:17,060
Today, copper and bronze are still used for decoration and jewelry.

109
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:25,080
Because copper is ductile or can be made into wire, and conductive, it is ideal for electrical wiring.

110
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:32,500
The strength of bronze makes it perfect for bearings, pinions, washers, and other important machine parts.

111
00:09:32,501 --> 00:09:39,740
But the age when bronze dominated forged metals ended, when a better, more plentiful element supplanted it.

112
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:42,040
That metal was iron.

113
00:09:45,820 --> 00:09:50,520
All United States nickels, dimes, and quarters are made of copper or copper alloys.

114
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:55,360
A thin metal cladding of copper and nickel provides their silvery shine.

115
00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:58,980
Metal will return on Modern Marvels.

116
00:10:02,500 --> 00:10:05,500
We now return to metal on modern marvels.

117
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:11,500
Iron, the material that supports our civilization.

118
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:18,500
Its age began approximately 2,500 years ago throughout Europe, Asia and Africa.

119
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:23,500
A period in which it superseded the alloy bronze.

120
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:28,500
Acquiring copper and especially tin, which were not always found in proximity to each other,

121
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:31,500
proved difficult as their value increased.

122
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:38,160
At that point, people had become quite reliant on bronze as a material to improve their lives.

123
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:43,760
And so when this was interrupted and they couldn't get the valuable tin to make the bronze, they turned to iron.

124
00:10:46,160 --> 00:10:49,480
Although iron was softer than bronze, it was more plentiful.

125
00:10:50,840 --> 00:10:53,320
The fourth most abundant element on Earth.

126
00:10:54,220 --> 00:10:56,880
An early source of iron was extraterrestrial.

127
00:10:57,740 --> 00:10:59,880
meteorites that had penetrated Earth's atmosphere,

128
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:02,600
giving the metal a supernatural aura.

129
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:07,000
Early man must have observed the meteors in the sky,

130
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:10,200
found the metal on the ground, and put two and two together.

131
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:15,840
The Egyptians had a word, bon e pet, which meant iron from heaven.

132
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:18,600
And the Assyrians, the Hebrews, and so forth,

133
00:11:18,601 --> 00:11:20,940
all had words in the vocabulary that described that.

134
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:25,680
Iron was so important to human progress

135
00:11:25,681 --> 00:11:30,020
that it would become the most important structural metal for roughly two millennia.

136
00:11:32,040 --> 00:11:34,320
Men worked iron in the blacksmith shop,

137
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:37,960
a place of fiery and almost mystical transformation,

138
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:42,780
where seemingly useless rocks become vital tools.

139
00:11:43,720 --> 00:11:46,300
The fact that they used fire and they were in control of fire

140
00:11:46,301 --> 00:11:49,880
probably brought them to religious status in a lot of ways.

141
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:54,420
That tradition survives today.

142
00:11:54,421 --> 00:11:57,660
at specialized shops and educational programs,

143
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:00,840
like Southern Illinois University's metalsmithing program.

144
00:12:01,960 --> 00:12:03,600
Some of the equipment is modern,

145
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:07,020
but the methods and the materials go back thousands of years.

146
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:11,440
I have iron ore in this pile and in this rock form,

147
00:12:12,040 --> 00:12:14,240
and I have limestone right here,

148
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:16,980
and it's basically just road gravel,

149
00:12:17,240 --> 00:12:20,460
like you would find on a gravel road in most parts of the country.

150
00:12:20,840 --> 00:12:23,380
We're going to crush the iron ore up, as you see,

151
00:12:23,381 --> 00:12:26,620
and that then will be put in the crucible.

152
00:12:27,820 --> 00:12:30,440
And crushing, very simple process.

153
00:12:34,020 --> 00:12:37,440
This gets loaded into our crucible.

154
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:46,440
And the limestone, this will melt at a lower temperature than the iron will,

155
00:12:46,700 --> 00:12:49,040
and it will cover it and control oxidation

156
00:12:49,041 --> 00:12:51,840
so that we reduce the materials down to small nodules.

157
00:12:53,380 --> 00:13:00,880
The limestone is called a flux, a substance that acts like a sealant, protecting the metal from oxidation when it melts.

158
00:13:03,620 --> 00:13:07,280
A heat-resistant ceramic crucible is then placed in a furnace.

159
00:13:09,560 --> 00:13:13,700
Gas ovens speed the process that in antiquity would have taken about 12 hours.

160
00:13:13,701 --> 00:13:25,340
In less than two, the material at roughly 2,700 degrees Fahrenheit bubbles out of the crucible as it forms a bloom.

161
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:36,660
As the mixture is poured out, the flux separates from the hot, spongy iron.

162
00:13:36,661 --> 00:13:48,040
Then we will take that spongy mass and hammer it into a recognizable shape that we see ironed in a lot of times.

163
00:13:49,860 --> 00:13:51,380
And these represent that.

164
00:13:54,740 --> 00:13:57,640
But this is only midpoint in the metal's transformation.

165
00:13:58,780 --> 00:14:03,780
The next step for the ancient blacksmiths would have been to make these wrought iron pieces into something useful,

166
00:14:04,420 --> 00:14:06,540
using traditional and ancient tools.

167
00:14:06,660 --> 00:14:13,920
the bellows to increase the flow of oxygen, raising the temperature of the fire,

168
00:14:15,460 --> 00:14:21,740
the anvil, the surface on which to place the metal, and the hammer with which to pound it.

169
00:14:21,741 --> 00:14:24,737
Now this is what's called a billet.

170
00:14:24,738 --> 00:14:29,360
It's just separate pieces of iron that will be stacked

171
00:14:29,361 --> 00:14:33,665
together and in the fire will be fused together by called forge welding.

172
00:14:33,666 --> 00:14:35,160
When it's lightly tapped,

173
00:14:35,161 --> 00:14:38,380
the molecules start to mesh between the two.

174
00:14:40,020 --> 00:14:42,760
And then as you forge it a little longer and a little longer,

175
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:45,800
it becomes one piece instead of multiple pieces.

176
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:54,180
If the right temperature is reached, characterized by an even yellow glow,

177
00:14:54,820 --> 00:14:57,460
the billet will more easily fuse together with hammering.

178
00:14:59,240 --> 00:15:03,240
If we get overzealous, it can easily split apart.

179
00:15:03,241 --> 00:15:07,920
This oxide is just a scale that's forming at high temperatures.

180
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:13,780
So by brushing it, I get a cleaner surface.

181
00:15:14,920 --> 00:15:19,000
Once the surface is brushed, the Smith sprinkles the billet with borax.

182
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:25,940
The borax also acts as a flux, forming a glassy sealant on the hot iron billet and preventing more oxidation.

183
00:15:27,860 --> 00:15:31,860
Hammering slowly shapes the metal, in this case into an axe head.

184
00:15:31,861 --> 00:15:34,051
This tool is called a drift.

185
00:15:34,052 --> 00:15:40,860
The drift just basically opens up a hole and turns it into whatever shape the drift is.

186
00:15:40,861 --> 00:15:41,561
Okay?

187
00:15:41,562 --> 00:15:52,860
So, by driving this in here, it's gradually spreading it out and shaping the size of the hole.

188
00:15:52,861 --> 00:15:59,860
A few more hits and it's done.

189
00:15:59,861 --> 00:16:07,860
From crushed stone to useful tool within the seemingly supernatural realm of the blacksmith.

190
00:16:07,861 --> 00:16:12,860
Other metals were valued for precisely the opposite reason that iron was.

191
00:16:12,861 --> 00:16:17,860
Gold is one of the rarest elements and least utilitarian because of its softness.

192
00:16:17,861 --> 00:16:21,660
But its yellow luster has captivated all cultures that have encountered it.

193
00:16:22,840 --> 00:16:23,540
The sun material.

194
00:16:23,900 --> 00:16:26,180
It was sort of an affirmative material.

195
00:16:26,780 --> 00:16:28,160
It was an optimistic material.

196
00:16:28,161 --> 00:16:32,940
The most important gods were either made of gold or covered with gold

197
00:16:32,941 --> 00:16:36,480
to symbol their superiority to lesser gods.

198
00:16:37,720 --> 00:16:40,240
Gold doesn't oxidize or tarnish readily,

199
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:44,500
is malleable, and melts at a relatively low temperature.

200
00:16:44,501 --> 00:16:48,400
In other words, it's perfect as a decoration and for coinage.

201
00:16:49,260 --> 00:16:53,300
In fact, gold was the perfect foundation for monetary systems throughout the world.

202
00:16:54,720 --> 00:17:00,420
It was a symbol of strength, of wealth, of political power.

203
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:06,480
It has this sort of mystique about it as being a symbol of success.

204
00:17:07,680 --> 00:17:11,320
Like copper, gold was first discovered in its native state,

205
00:17:11,321 --> 00:17:14,520
shining from surface deposits or in rich veins.

206
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:21,360
The Egyptians in Nubia, which is a rich gold area,

207
00:17:21,361 --> 00:17:22,980
which is now part of Sudan,

208
00:17:23,220 --> 00:17:26,620
were the first ones to develop significant ability

209
00:17:26,621 --> 00:17:28,280
to do underground tunnel mining.

210
00:17:28,580 --> 00:17:31,940
And they would dig up entire rooms and create ventilation systems

211
00:17:31,941 --> 00:17:35,580
and use primitive methods to bring the ore up.

212
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:40,620
The search for gold spurred colonization of the New World

213
00:17:40,621 --> 00:17:45,160
in the 16th century, particularly among the Spanish conquistadors in the Americas.

214
00:17:46,560 --> 00:17:52,140
Although they never found the fabled El Dorado, or Lost City of Gold, they managed to find

215
00:17:52,141 --> 00:17:56,980
and steal enough to make Spain, for the time, the most wealthy of the European powers.

216
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:04,400
As explorers set out across the oceans in search of gold, early European metallurgists

217
00:18:04,401 --> 00:18:06,860
struggled to find a way to magically create it.

218
00:18:06,861 --> 00:18:10,680
You might have heard of the alchemists back in the older days.

219
00:18:11,100 --> 00:18:14,180
There was this field of science where people wanted to turn everything into gold.

220
00:18:14,440 --> 00:18:16,780
And really this is kind of the field that led to chemistry.

221
00:18:18,280 --> 00:18:24,720
Alchemy, a complex mix of physics and metaphysics, arrived in Europe as early as the 9th century.

222
00:18:26,180 --> 00:18:32,240
Alchemists were preoccupied with the essential nature of substances and their ability to transform into other ones.

223
00:18:32,241 --> 00:18:37,380
Some believed that everything was made of small particles with hooks on them

224
00:18:37,381 --> 00:18:39,720
that helped them connect with other particles.

225
00:18:41,980 --> 00:18:43,780
Rearrange and renumber the connections,

226
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:47,820
and you could, according to the alchemists, recreate elements,

227
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:53,840
possibly out of combinations of mercury and sulfur, or lead.

228
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:59,260
Their premise of transmutating other materials into gold,

229
00:18:59,261 --> 00:19:05,100
this idea of creating the riches as opposed to mining or finding or conquering for them,

230
